---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import PixelHeart from '../components/PixelHeart.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url';

const evaluationQuestions = [
  {
    title: 'Does it protect the floor?',
    body: 'Does this work strengthen physical, mental or social health — or at minimum, refuse to spend it? Work that quietly borrows against the foundation of the people doing it fails the first test, whatever else it achieves.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Does it enrich the games layer?',
    body: 'Does it make good pursuits more available, more learnable or more joyful — for the people involved, or for others? The optional layer is where the value of a life accumulates; work that widens it counts.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Is it honest about its stage?',
    body: 'Eshkere would rather fund, build or support something that states its uncertainty than something that performs maturity. This website holds itself to the same rule.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Would we play it wholeheartedly?',
    body: 'A project is itself a game: chosen, rule-bound, stoppable. If nobody involved would choose it freely and play it with real appetite, that is evidence worth taking seriously.',
  },
];

const openQuestions = [
  'Where exactly the line runs between a health need and a chosen pursuit — work that pays for food is both.',
  'What form Eshkere itself should take: a company, a fund, a studio, a community, or something else.',
  'How to measure the foundation — across physical, mental and social well-being — without turning measurement into another anxious game.',
  'Which projects come first, and what proportion of effort belongs to protecting the floor versus enriching the layer above it.',
  'What Eshkere owes the public good beyond the people directly involved in its work.',
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Our Approach"
  description="The Maximize Health, Play Games thesis in full: what health means, what games means, why the order matters, and which questions Eshkere has deliberately not answered yet."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Our Approach"
    title="Maximize Health, Play Games"
    lead="One sentence carries the whole approach. This page unpacks it honestly — including the parts that are still undecided."
  />

  <article class="approach">
    <!-- What health means -->
    <section class="approach__block container--narrow" aria-labelledby="health-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 40 12" width="40" height="12" aria-hidden="true" class="pulse-line">
          <polyline points="0,7 12,7 16,1 21,11 25,4 28,7 40,7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round"></polyline>
        </svg>
        Health
      </p>
      <h2 id="health-heading">What health means</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Health, in the Eshkere sense, is the complete non-optional layer of a human
          life: <strong>physical</strong> well-being — sleep, movement, nourishment, a body that
          works; <strong>mental</strong> well-being — attention, mood, a mind you can live in;
          and <strong>social</strong> well-being — connection, belonging, people who know you.
        </p>
        <p>
          These are not three lifestyle preferences. They are the conditions under which every other
          human pursuit either works or degrades.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Why non-optional -->
    <section class="approach__block container--narrow" aria-labelledby="nonoptional-heading">
      <h2 id="nonoptional-heading">Why health is non-optional</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          You can quit chess. You can quit a career, a competition, a scene. You cannot quit
          needing sleep, food, movement, connection or a functioning mind — you can only defer the
          bill. That asymmetry is the entire basis of the ordering: health is serious precisely
          because it concerns needs that cannot be opted out of.
        </p>
        <p>
          When the foundation erodes, it does not subtract one pursuit from a life. It taxes all of
          them at once — thinking, patience, play, love. This is why Eshkere refuses to treat health
          as one goal competing on a list. It is the surface the list is written on.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- What games means -->
    <section class="approach__block container--narrow" aria-labelledby="games-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--pink">
        <PixelHeart size={14} color="currentColor" /> Games
      </p>
      <h2 id="games-heading">What games means</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A game is any pursuit a person can genuinely choose: it has stakes you opted into, rules
          you can learn, progress you can feel, and the freedom to stop. By that definition, games
          include careers and chess, friendships and research programs, art, argument, status,
          exploration, gardening and worship.
        </p>
        <p>
          Eshkere borrows the visual language of video games playfully — pixels, hearts, level-ups —
          but the category is far wider than software. <strong>Games are everything humans can
          choose to pursue once the foundation exists</strong>: enjoyment, achievement, identity,
          exploration, status, creativity, relationships, knowledge, meaning and play.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Why games remain valuable -->
    <section class="approach__block container--narrow" aria-labelledby="valuable-heading">
      <h2 id="valuable-heading">Why games remain valuable</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Calling these pursuits optional is not a demotion. Almost everything people cherish about
          being alive lives in the optional layer — meaning, mastery, beauty, belonging,
          achievement. Nobody secures a foundation for its own sake; the point of the floor is the
          dancing that happens on it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The claim is only about <em>ordering</em>. Games played on a crumbling foundation go flat:
          the exhausted player is not freed from their games, just condemned to play them badly.
          Health first is what makes wholehearted play possible at all.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <hr class="rule-iridescent container--narrow" />

    <!-- Evaluation -->
    <section class="approach__block approach__block--wide container" aria-labelledby="evaluate-heading">
      <div class="approach__wide-head container--narrow">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--lime">Evaluation</p>
        <h2 id="evaluate-heading">How Eshkere would evaluate possible projects</h2>
        <p class="approach__wide-lead">
          No project has been formally evaluated yet — the portfolio is honest about that. But the
          thesis already implies the questions any candidate would face:
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="approach__criteria">
        {
          evaluationQuestions.map((question, index) => (
            <article class="approach__criterion panel">
              <p class="approach__criterion-number">{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</p>
              <h3>{question.title}</h3>
              <p>{question.body}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Wellbeing, public good, exploration, play -->
    <section class="approach__block container--narrow" aria-labelledby="relation-heading">
      <h2 id="relation-heading">Well-being, public good, exploration and play</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          These four are usually pitched against each other — serious welfare work over here,
          frivolous play over there. The Eshkere thesis treats them as one structure. Well-being is
          the foundation. The public good is what happens when foundations are secured at scale,
          for people beyond yourself. Exploration and play are not the reward that comes after the
          serious work; they are the point of it, and often the method too — most discoveries look
          like somebody playing.
        </p>
        <p>
          How exactly Eshkere balances its own well-being work against the wider public good is one
          of the open questions below. The commitment that already exists is the ordering itself:
          no game, however glittering, gets to quietly consume the foundation it stands on.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Open questions -->
    <section class="approach__block container--narrow approach__open" aria-labelledby="open-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--pink">Undecided</p>
      <h2 id="open-heading">Questions we have deliberately not answered</h2>
      <p class="approach__open-lead">
        Eshkere is an early-stage exploration. Pretending these are settled would be a lie the rest
        of this site works hard not to tell:
      </p>
      <ul class="approach__open-list">
        {openQuestions.map((question) => <li>{question}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p class="approach__open-note">
        As positions form, they will be argued
        in <a href={url('/research-and-news')}>Research &amp; News</a> — uncertainty first,
        conclusions second.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .approach {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--section-gap);
    padding-block: var(--section-gap) 0;
  }

  .approach__block {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.1rem;
  }

  .approach__block h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
  }

  .approach__block .eyebrow {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
  }

  .pulse-line {
    color: inherit;
  }

  .approach__wide-head {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.1rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .approach__wide-lead {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .approach__criteria {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .approach__criterion {
    padding: 1.75rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    align-content: start;
  }

  .approach__criterion-number {
    font-family: var(--font-pixel);
    color: var(--lime);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
  }

  .approach__criterion h3 {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
  }

  .approach__criterion p:last-child {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .approach__open {
    border: 1px solid var(--line-soft);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    background:
      radial-gradient(30rem 18rem at 90% 0%, rgba(255, 79, 200, 0.06), transparent 70%),
      rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.02);
  }

  .approach__open-lead {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .approach__open-list {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.9rem;
    padding-left: 1.3rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .approach__open-list li::marker {
    color: var(--pink);
  }

  .approach__open-note {
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
</style>
